# What CPython prints for both lines here (python3, 3.12):
#
#   0
#   0.0
#
# `int()` and `float()` with no argument are the conversions of zero, which is what the reference
# answers — and neither engine gets there. The interpreter reaches for `Args[0]` before it has asked
# whether there is one, so the program dies with a Go runtime panic and **exit 2**, the code the
# exit-code contract reserves for a compiler bug (ADR 0166). The compiled backend refuses with
# `int expects one argument` and exit 1, which is the code for "your program has a compile error" —
# issued here for a program CPython runs in one line (roadmap L11.8's complaint exactly).
#
# `round()` was the fifth member of this family and answers an arity error on both engines now
# (ADR 0263). `ord()` and `chr()` panic the same way on programs CPython itself rejects, and `chr()`
# panics the compiler as well. Owner: the same arity check in front of every builtin that takes an
# argument (roadmap Gap R.131).
print(int())
print(float())
